{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations, etc.</b> It is necessary to know: (1) in the place where it mentions the generations of Yitzhak, it mentions the birth of Yitzhak; (2) there was no need to inform us that Avraham begot Yitzhak." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of the verse is along this path: <b>And these are the generations of Yitzhak</b> — meaning those mentioned at the end of the parashah. And since it mentions the sons of Yitzhak, and it has already preceded us that Yitzhak when he was born was not on a level to beget, for he came from the side of the feminine (סטרא דנוקבא), as we have written (Genesis 22:2) — if so, from where does he have generations? Therefore it concluded: <b>Avraham begot Yitzhak</b> — meaning, he activated the power of generation in Yitzhak, by drawing upon him a soul that is on the level of the one who begets, and this was by means of the Binding (Akeidah), as we have written." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Yevamot 64a): ״And HaShem was entreated of him״ — because nothing compares to the prayer of a righteous son of a righteous man, end quote. From this you learn that on account of his having a righteous father, HaShem was favorably disposed to him and gave him children. And this is its saying <b>Avraham begot</b>, etc. — meaning, by virtue of the merit of Avraham, he begot, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to their words of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 63) on the verse (Isaiah 29:22): ״concerning the house of Yaakov, who redeemed Avraham״ — for in the merit of Yaakov, Avraham was saved from the fiery furnace, and without this Avraham would have, Heaven forbid, been burned in the furnace of the Kasdim. And this is its saying <b>these are the generations of Yitzhak son of Avraham</b> — that by means of him [Yaakov], Avraham begot Yitzhak, for without Yaakov, who is the generations of Yitzhak, Avraham would already have been burned before he could beget Yitzhak." "<b>Further,</b> it hints at the essential generations of the righteous, which are commandments and good deeds. For since Yitzhak's trials and the parameters of his righteousness were not known, and even though he stretched out his neck upon the altar, this matter is not explicit in the Torah — and to this day there are commentators (Ibn Ezra 22:4) who say he was bound against his will — therefore the verse informs us by saying ״And these are the generations of Yitzhak son of Avraham״: look and see the one who begot and the one who was raised — like begets like. And it also hints in the word <b>son of Avraham</b> along the lines of the verse (Proverbs 10) ״a wise son״ — meaning that he [Yitzhak] is Avraham in the aspect of righteousness and the surpassing of his love, may He be blessed. Accordingly, it is also precise in its saying <b>And these</b> with the addition of the vav, to say: in addition to the generations of Avraham, who was renowned for good through many trials, also the generations of Yitzhak similarly." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in this manner to ״וְאֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדֹת יִצְחָק בֶּן״ — 'אַבְרָהָם was alone,' meaning, comparable to him, for the two sets of descendants of Yitzhak did not resemble those of Avraham." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in His saying <b>Avraham begot</b> etc., in this manner: that although the good descendants of Yitzhak resembled those of Avraham — as is alluded to by saying ״וְאֵלֶּה״ with the addition of the vav — despite all this, his merit did not equal that of Avraham, for he — Avraham — is the one who merited it for him. And that is what it says, <b>Avraham begot et Yitzhak</b> — the aspect of good within him came by virtue of the one who begot him, which was not so with Avraham, whose father was an idol worshipper, and he girded himself to attain his Creator, and the greatness of Avraham was exalted. And therefore he is called (Isaiah 41:8) 'Avraham who loved Him,' blessed be He — meaning: he preceded in loving his Maker; before HaShem would shine His love upon him, he pursued Him with wondrous self-strengthening, which was not so with Yitzhak, for Avraham built within him the structure of love for the good, and when he opened his eyes to the world, the spirit of HaShem already rested upon him." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in this manner, that the descendants of Yitzhak were in a measure more distinguished than the descendants of Avraham, for a righteous son of a righteous man is not comparable to a righteous son of a wicked man (Yevamot 64) — the latter being Avraham son of Terach. And although the Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 38:12) that he [Terach] repented, despite all this, from the time of Avraham's birth until close to his [Terach's] death he was only at the level of a wicked man, and the deed of a wicked father darkens the light of the son's soul. And in this respect the worthiness of Yitzhak's deeds was multiplied, and that is what it says <b>וְאֵלֶּה</b> — with addition to the first — and it gives the reason by saying ״Avraham begot״ etc.:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say in this manner: <b>These are the descendants</b> — meaning the events that befell Yitzhak, as explained in the order of the parashah, and as they said of blessed memory that all of them were complete without any event of suffering, and with abundance of good, wealth, and honor, as explained in the commentary of the Ramban of blessed memory (Genesis 25:34), which was not so for Avraham, through whom many troubles passed. And Scripture gave the reason, on the one hand, because Avraham begot et Yitzhak, and on the other hand, because his father was righteous and merited for him the wealth and the honor etc., which was not so with Avraham, who did not have a father to merit for him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:38.293845501Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}